            <td data-label="Who pays, and how they trust you">{row.pays}</td>
            <td data-label="How you enter">{row.entry}</td>
            <td data-label="Business layer">{row.layer}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Scalable ownership inside this degree:</strong> farm advisory, an input shop with a farm-advice desk, consulting for several FPOs, and a drone service across a cluster of villages all let you set the price, add repeat customers and later hire people. A pay scale does not. Each needs trust, a licence or capital, so spend a season or two inside a working unit before you start your own. Building that skill portfolio, with proof a farmer or an FPO can check, is the decision that unlocks high income opportunities.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A BSc Agriculture is a common degree. Over 28,000 students join agricultural degrees each year in the national system alone, so the degree by itself does not stand out. What stands out is one crop, one district and one result you can show.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you mainly want a high salary fast, data analysis, digital marketing or software scale faster for many people. Agriculture wins when you want the farm, the farmer and the field to be your work, and you are willing to build trust slowly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="exams">Exam doors, with the sums</h2>

  <p>
    A monthly pay number means little until you know how many posts exist and what comes after entry. Each row is drawn from a notice, an official page or a summary named in the sources. Unsourced pay ranges from student blogs are left out.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--money">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>Entry rule</th>
