          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public notices and summaries, not exact or guaranteed amounts. Pay differs by city, employer, skill and year, and markets change. Check current job listings and ask people working in the role before you decide. Several rows come from coaching or news summaries, so confirm each on the issuing body's own page.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Two filters, not one</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>ICAR research has an entry stage (the PG exam, a fellowship seat or the ASRB exam) and a later, separate stage for posts and promotions. Clearing the first does not clear the second.</li>
      <li>Bank and state posts have their own exam stages and counts that change from year to year. A year with fewer posts means a harder year, so treat any single count as a guide.</li>
      <li>A fellowship is training. It pays 55 percent of the entry scientist figure, and it does not carry a job with it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take on exam years</p>
    <p>
      Every attempt costs a year of earning. A student guide quotes about Rs 3 to 5 lakh a year as a private starting range, without an official source, so treat it as a rough price tag on one year spent only on preparation. Compare that with the fixed posts above, and keep a paying skill alive during any exam run. Posts also come with rules on outside work, transfers and slow pay growth, which differ by service, so read them before you commit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Think big on purpose. A post is a safe floor, and owning the advice, the shop or the farm is where income can outgrow a pay scale.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="layers">Four business layers you can own</h2>

  <p>
    "Start a business" is where most advice ends. For each layer below you get what it sells, the rule to check, one number to track and the usual trap. Reliable public set-up costs were not found, so none are printed. Ask three owners what theirs was.
  </p>

