            <td data-label="What stays with a person">{row.human}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Start now: use an app or chat tool to draft a crop plan, then check every number against a soil test, your university's package of practices and a field visit. Keep a log of what the tool got wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    Later, as tools and adoption mature, build a small workflow that turns drone or satellite images into a plot note a farmer can act on, and show it. No multiplier is guaranteed. A tool lets you serve more farms in the same hours, but only when you earn the farmer's trust first. The better alternatives for a student who mainly wants speed to income are data analysis or agri-tech sales, and both still reward farm knowledge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="money">Fees, free learning and the MSc question</h2>

  <p>
    Use a strict planning rule, not a law: about 10 percent of the total education budget for the degree, and a warning sign above roughly 20 percent unless that exact course proves its worth. On a Rs 8 lakh budget that means Rs 80,000 and Rs 1.6 lakh. The remaining money pays for travel to farms, a phone or laptop, a soil test kit, stock money and a second skill.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Here the degree works as a key. It opens bank, state and ICAR posts, many licences and college teaching. Crop planning, soil reading, spreadsheets, farm accounts, drone basics and clear speaking can be learned from university extension pages, KVK demonstration days, good YouTube channels and low-cost courses. Pay for a course only if it gives feedback or a credential an employer names.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before you pay for a short course or an MBA, ask who teaches it, when it was last updated, how many hours happen in a real field or shop, what the refund terms are and what you will have built by the end. Skip an education loan unless the credential is legally required. Many certificates do not add up to a skill portfolio.
    </p>
  </div>

